            <h2>Airlines Flying from Las Vegas to Honolulu</h2>
            <h3>Full-Service Airlines</h3>
            <h4>Southwest Airlines</h4>
            <p>As the dominant carrier at Harry Reid International and a major force in West Coast-Hawaii service, Southwest anchors this desert-to-island corridor with multiple daily frequencies and a passenger-friendly approach that resonates with Las Vegas locals and visitors alike.</p>
            <p><b>Highlights:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Daily nonstop flights on Boeing 737-800 and 737 MAX 8 aircraft certified for extended over-water ETOPS operations.</li>
                <li>Rapid Rewards earning with Companion Pass eligibility, A-List priority boarding, and no blackout dates on award redemptions</li>
                <li>Two free checked bags on every fare, a transformative advantage for golfers hauling club sets, surfers transporting boards, and families packing resort wardrobes</li>
                <li>No change fees and no cancellation penalties, offering flexibility when desert heat waves or island storms disrupt plans</li>
                <li>Open seating policy allowing groups to sit together without advance seat selection surcharges</li>
                <li>Extensive domestic network feeding Las Vegas from across the Southwest and Mountain West for connecting Honolulu itineraries</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Hawaiian Airlines</h4>
            <p>The Aloha State's flag carrier brings authentic island hospitality to this desert crossing, positioning itself as the only carrier headquartered in the destination state.</p>
            <p><b>Advantages:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Daily nonstop flights aboard Airbus A321neo or A330-200 aircraft with island-inspired cabin design and crew trained in Hawaiian cultural protocols</li>
                <li>HawaiianMiles earning with Pualani elite status, companion fare discounts, and valuable partner redemption options</li>
                <li>Extra Comfort seating offering priority boarding, enhanced legroom, and personal power outlets at a moderate upgrade cost</li>
                <li>Complimentary island-inspired meals even in Main Cabin, featuring Hawaiian sweet bread, island-style entrees, and tropical beverages</li>
                <li>Strong cultural immersion beginning at boarding with Hawaiian language announcements, lei-inspired cabin aesthetics, and island music</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Delta Air Lines</h4>
            <p>Delta routes Las Vegas passengers through its Los Angeles, Salt Lake City, and Seattle hubs, with Los Angeles offering the most direct western routing to Honolulu.</p>
            <p><b>Advantages:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>SkyMiles earning with Medallion qualifying segments and dollars toward elite tier status.</li>
                <li>Delta One business class with fully flat seats, premium dining, and Westin Heavenly Inflight bedding on select long-haul configured aircraft</li>
                <li>Reliable operational metrics and proactive rebooking during irregular operations, particularly important for vacation-sensitive itineraries</li>
                <li>Strong codeshare and inter-island connections through Hawaiian Airlines partnership</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>United Airlines</h4>
            <p>United connects Las Vegas travelers through its San Francisco, Denver, and Los Angeles hubs, with San Francisco offering the most direct coastal routing to Honolulu.</p>
            <p><b>Features:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>MileagePlus earning with Premier qualifying credits and lifetime miles accumulation.</li>
                <li>Polaris Business Class with lie-flat beds, Saks Fifth Avenue bedding, and premium dining on long-haul segments</li>
                <li>Star Alliance membership providing global lounge access and reciprocal benefits.</li>
                <li>Strong connecting schedule through San Francisco with onward nonstop service to HNL</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Alaska Airlines</h4>
            <p>The Seattle-based carrier connects Las Vegas travelers through its Pacific Northwest hubs, appealing to Mileage Plan loyalists throughout the Southwest and California.</p>
            <p><b>Benefits:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Mileage Plan earning with valuable elite status tiers and generous partner redemption opportunities</li>
                <li>First Class cabin with premium dining and complimentary West Coast craft beer and wine</li>
                <li>Strong Pacific network providing alternative routing through Portland, Seattle, and San Francisco</li>
                <li>Partnership with Hawaiian Airlines for inter-island connections and reciprocal earning</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>American Airlines</h4>
            <p>American routes Las Vegas-Honolulu passengers through its Phoenix, Los Angeles, and Dallas-Fort Worth hubs, with Phoenix and Los Angeles offering the most direct southwestern routing to Oahu.</p>
            <p><b>Benefits:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>AAdvantage mileage earning with elite qualifying dollars, segments, and lifetime miles accumulation</li>
                <li>Multiple cabin experiences spanning Main Cabin, Main Cabin Extra, Premium Economy, and Flagship Business on wide-body aircraft</li>
                <li>oneworld alliance membership providing global lounge access and reciprocal benefits</li>
                <li>Strong connecting schedule through Phoenix with favorable weather reliability during summer monsoon season</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Low-Cost Options</h3>
            <p>No ultra-low-cost carrier currently operates nonstop from Las Vegas to Honolulu. Spirit and Frontier serve Las Vegas extensively for mainland destinations but do not cross the Pacific. Budget travelers monitor Southwest sales and legacy carrier promotional fares, particularly during shoulder season.</p>
            <p><b>Inter-island budget carriers serving Oahu:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Southwest - </b>Expanding inter-island network connecting Honolulu to Kahului, Kona, and Lihue</li>
                <li><b>Mokulele Airlines - </b>Commuter service using small turboprop aircraft for short island hops</li>
            </ul>
            <h2>Seasonal Variations</h2>
            <h3>Peak Season: December to March and June to August</h3>
            <p>Highest demand with corresponding premium pricing.</p>
            <h4>During peak months:</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Airfares reach annual highs, particularly Christmas week, New Year's, Presidents' Day weekend, and mid-July</li>
                <li>Southwest and Hawaiian operate daily nonstops at maximum frequency, sometimes adding additional seasonal capacity</li>
                <li>Resort rates in Waikiki, Ko Olina, and Turtle Bay reach premium levels with minimum stay requirements</li>
                <li>Rental car inventory sells out months in advance, particularly for convertibles, jeeps, and SUVs</li>
                <li>Popular activities including Pearl Harbor USS Arizona Memorial, Diamond Head summit hikes, and North Shore surf competitions require planning</li>
                <li>Restaurant availability becomes challenging at waterfront establishments in Haleiwa, Kailua, and Waikiki</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Premium pricing periods:</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><b>December 20 through January 5 - </b>absolute peak as Las Vegans escape desert winter and holiday chaos</li>
                <li><b>Presidents' Day and MLK Day weekends -</b> short-break demand from Henderson and Summerlin</li>
                <li><b>June through August -</b> family travel aligned with Nevada school schedules and escape from triple-digit heat</li>
                <li><b>Thanksgiving week -</b> popular pre-holiday tropical escape</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Shoulder Season: April to May and September to November</h3>
            <p>Most balanced Oahu experience with pleasant weather and manageable crowds.</p>
            <p><b>Advantages:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Economy fares typically 20-35% below December peaks</li>
                <li>Temperatures in the 70s to 80s°F with reliable trade winds and occasional light 	showers on the windward side</li>
                <li>Better availability at boutique hotels, vacation rentals, and resort properties</li>
                <li>Smaller crowds at Hanauma Bay, Diamond Head, and the Polynesian Cultural Center</li>
                <li>Ideal conditions for road-tripping the Windward Coast, exploring Kailua Town, and snorkeling Shark's Cove</li>
                <li>Shoulder season surf on the North Shore begins building in September and October</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Off-Season: Late August to October (excluding Labor Day)</h3>
            <p>Late summer and early autumn offer value before winter demand surges.</p>
            <p><b>Benefits:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Lowest airfares of the year - economy round-trips frequently $350-$600</li>
                <li>Reduced tourist presence at major beaches and attractions</li>
                <li>Hotel rates at annual lows with promotional packages and resort credits</li>
                <li>Warm ocean temperatures ideal for snorkeling, diving, and beginner surfing</li>
                <li>Hurricane season runs June through November, though direct hits on Oahu are rare; travel insurance recommended</li>
            </ul>
            <p><b>Considerations:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Higher humidity and occasional tropical showers, particularly on the Windward Coast</li>
                <li>Some resort restaurants and activities reduce hours in late August and September</li>
                <li>Hurricane season requires flexible planning and comprehensive travel insurance</li>
            </ul>
