            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Q1: What is the cheapest time to fly from Las Vegas to Honolulu?</h3>
            <p>Late August through October offers the lowest fares, typically $350 to $600 round-trip. For winter escapes, book 60 to 90 days ahead and target January post-holiday. Tuesday and Wednesday departures consistently undercut weekend pricing throughout the year.</p>

            <h3>Q2: Are there direct flights from Las Vegas to Honolulu?</h3>
            <p>Southwest Airlines and Hawaiian Airlines both operate daily year-round nonstop flights from Las Vegas to Honolulu. Southwest offers multiple daily frequencies. Flight duration is approximately six hours across the Pacific.</p>

            <h3>Q3: Do I need a passport to visit Honolulu from Las Vegas?</h3>
            <p>No passport is required for United States citizens traveling to Honolulu. Hawaii is the 50th state, so standard domestic travel rules apply. A government-issued photo ID or driver's license suffices for TSA screening at Harry Reid International Airport.</p>

            <h3>Q4: How much baggage can I bring on a Las Vegas to Honolulu flight?</h3>
            <p>Southwest allows two free checked bags. Most other carriers charge $35 for the first checked bag up to 50 pounds, plus a carry-on and personal item. Surfboards and golf clubs may incur additional fees on Hawaiian, United, and Delta but fly free on Southwest.</p>

            <h3>Q5: What is the best way to get from Honolulu Airport to Waikiki?</h3>
            <p>The Bus Route 20 costs $3 and reaches Waikiki in 45 to 60 minutes. SpeediShuttle shared rides cost $15 to $18. Taxis and Uber run $35 to $50 and take 20 to 35 minutes depending on H-1 freeway traffic conditions.</p>

            <h3>Q6: Can I island-hop after flying into Honolulu from Las Vegas?</h3>
            <p>Yes, inter-island flights connect Honolulu to Maui, Kauai, and the Big Island in 25 to 45 minutes. Hawaiian Airlines and Southwest operate frequent island-hopper service. No additional TSA screening is required for connecting flights within Hawaii.</p>

            <h3>Q7: Is Honolulu safe for tourists from Las Vegas?</h3>
            <p>Honolulu is generally very safe, though petty theft occurs at beaches and tourist areas. Ocean safety requires more attention than crime; strong currents, rip tides, and sharp coral pose real risks. Always heed warning flags and lifeguard instructions at all beaches.</p>

            <h3>Q8: What is the best area to stay in Honolulu for first-time visitors?</h3>
            <p>Waikiki offers the most accessible introduction with beaches, restaurants, and Diamond Head views. Ko Olina provides resort tranquility on the Leeward Coast. Kailua suits those seeking a local vibe with Lanikai Beach. Downtown Honolulu works for history enthusiasts near Iolani Palace.</p>

            <h3>Q9: How early should I arrive at Harry Reid International for my Honolulu flight?</h3>
            <p>Arrive two hours before scheduled departure for domestic flights from LAS. During holiday weekends and convention weeks, consider arriving two and a half hours early. TSA PreCheck and CLEAR members can significantly reduce security wait times.</p>

            <h3>Q10: What happens if my flight to Honolulu is delayed or cancelled?</h3>
            <p>Airlines must rebook passengers on the next available flight at no extra cost for weather or operational delays. Unlike international routes, EU compensation rules do not apply. Maintain comprehensive travel insurance for accommodation and activity reimbursement protection.</p>


















